---
import Downloads from "../components/Downloads.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { href, source, repository, releaseVersion, releaseAsset } from "../data/project";
const windowsDemo = `.\\agent-lens.exe rules-check --rules .\\examples\\rules\\default.yaml
.\\agent-lens.exe demo --output .\\demo.jsonl
Get-Content .\\demo.jsonl`;
const posixDemo = `./agent-lens rules-check --rules ./examples/rules/default.yaml
./agent-lens demo --output ./demo.jsonl
cat ./demo.jsonl`;
const windowsStart = `$lensState = Join-Path $PWD 'lens-state'
$lensOutput = Join-Path $PWD 'events.jsonl'
$lensRules = Join-Path $PWD 'examples\\rules\\default.yaml'
.\\agent-lens.exe --state $lensState start --output $lensOutput --rules $lensRules
.\\agent-lens.exe --state $lensState config --agent claude --shell powershell
.\\agent-lens.exe --state $lensState status`;
const posixStart = `lens_state="$PWD/lens-state"
./agent-lens --state "$lens_state" start \\
  --output "$PWD/events.jsonl" \\
  --rules "$PWD/examples/rules/default.yaml"
./agent-lens --state "$lens_state" config --agent claude
./agent-lens --state "$lens_state" status`;
---

<Layout
  title="Getting started — AI Lens"
  active="start"
  description="Download AI Lens for Windows, Linux, or macOS. Run the synthetic demo, review native hook configuration, and collect local JSONL events."
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">FIELD GUIDE / 01</p>
      <h1>
        Get a clearer view.
        <br />
        <span>Start locally.</span>
      </h1>
      <p>
        Run a synthetic demo first. Then connect a supported agent, review its
        hooks, and verify what reaches your event stream.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="docs-layout">
      <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <span>ON THIS PAGE</span>
        <a href="#downloads">01 / Download & extract</a>
        <a href="#demo">02 / Run the demo</a>
        <a href="#connect">03 / Connect an agent</a>
        <a href="#gemini">Gemini CLI</a>
        <a href="#cowork">Cowork</a>
        <a href="#evidence">04 / Collect evidence</a>
        <a href="#stop">05 / Stop collection</a>
        <a href="#source">Build from source</a>
      </aside>
      <div class="docs-content">
        <section class="doc-section" id="downloads">
          <p class="eyebrow">01 / DOWNLOAD & EXTRACT</p>
          <h2>Ready for your platform.</h2>
          <p>Download the ZIP for your OS and architecture. The command remains
            <code>agent-lens</code> for compatibility. Extract the whole package;
            the executable needs its adjacent engine library.</p>
          <Downloads />
          <details class="command-details">
            <summary>Verify your download · SHA-256</summary>
            <p>Download <a href={releaseAsset("SHA256SUMS")}>SHA256SUMS</a> and compare
              your ZIP's hash with the entry for that exact filename. They must match
              before you extract and run it.</p>
            <CodeBlock code={`Get-FileHash "$HOME\\Downloads\\agent-lens-${releaseVersion}-windows-x86_64.zip" -Algorithm SHA256`} lang="powershell" label="Windows · PowerShell" />
            <CodeBlock code={`sha256sum "$HOME/Downloads/agent-lens-${releaseVersion}-linux-x86_64.zip"`} label="Linux · shell" />
            <CodeBlock code={`shasum -a 256 "$HOME/Downloads/agent-lens-${releaseVersion}-macos-aarch64.zip"`} label="macOS · shell" />
          </details>
          <details class="command-details" open>
            <summary>Windows · extract with PowerShell</summary>
            <CodeBlock code={`Expand-Archive "$HOME\\Downloads\\agent-lens-${releaseVersion}-windows-x86_64.zip" -DestinationPath "$HOME\\AI-Lens"
cd "$HOME\\AI-Lens\\agent-lens-${releaseVersion}-windows-x86_64"`} lang="powershell" label="PowerShell · after downloading" />
          </details>
          {[
            { name: "Linux x64", platform: "linux-x86_64" },
            { name: "macOS Apple Silicon", platform: "macos-aarch64" },
          ].map(({ name, platform }) => (
            <details class="command-details" open>
              <summary>{name} · extract in a shell</summary>
              <CodeBlock code={`package="$HOME/Downloads/agent-lens-${releaseVersion}-${platform}.zip"
mkdir -p "$HOME/AI-Lens"
unzip "$package" -d "$HOME/AI-Lens"
cd "$HOME/AI-Lens/$(basename "$package" .zip)"
chmod +x ./agent-lens`} label={`${name} · after downloading`} />
            </details>
          ))}
          <p class="small-note">The macOS package is unsigned and not notarized.
            If macOS blocks it, review <a href="https://support.apple.com/102445">Apple's downloaded-app approval guidance</a>
            in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security. Approve only a download you trust.</p>
          <div class="callout">
            <strong>Seven native adapters. Eleven telemetry sources.</strong>
            <p>Claude Code, Codex, Hermes, Gemini CLI, Cursor, Copilot CLI and Cowork
              use native hooks. <a href={href("telemetry/#native")}>Review client setup and limits</a>.
              OTLP and provider audit ingestion are observation-only.
              Antigravity is unsupported.</p>
          </div>
          <p>
            Packages contain an executable and adjacent engine library:
            <code>agent_lens_engine.dll</code> on Windows,
            <code>libagent_lens_engine.so</code> on Linux, and
            <code>libagent_lens_engine.dylib</code> on macOS.
            Keep the bundled <code>examples/</code> folder for the demo and default rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            Windows x64 builds require the latest <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/windows/latest-supported-vc-redist#latest-supported-redistributable-version">Microsoft Visual C++ v14 x64 Redistributable</a>.
            The ZIP does not bundle it. A missing <code>VCRUNTIME140.dll</code>
            prevents Lens from starting and returning hook decisions.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="doc-section" id="demo">
          <p class="eyebrow">02 / NO AGENT ACCOUNT NEEDED</p>
          <h2>Make the first event synthetic.</h2>
          <p>
            From the extracted package folder, validate the default policy and
            run the demo. It appends five synthetic events; it does not install
            hooks or execute the illustrated tool operations.
          </p>
          <details class="command-details" open>
            <summary>Windows · PowerShell</summary>
            <CodeBlock
              code={windowsDemo}
              lang="powershell"
              label="PowerShell · extracted package"
            />
          </details>
          <details class="command-details" open>
            <summary>Linux & macOS · POSIX shell</summary>
            <CodeBlock code={posixDemo} label="Shell · extracted package" />
          </details>
          <p>
            Demo events include <code>synthetic: true</code> and redacted sample
            content. Normal collection omits raw prompts and tool content by
            default. Reusing the output file appends another five records.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="doc-section" id="connect">
          <p class="eyebrow">03 / MANUAL CONFIGURATION</p>
          <h2>Connect an agent deliberately.</h2>
          <p>
            Use one absolute state path in both management commands and
            generated hooks. Output and rule paths below resolve from the
            extracted package folder; the output parent must already exist.
          </p>
          <p>
            AI Lens does not detect running agents or install settings. You
            choose the project or user configuration file and merge the printed
            entries yourself. No Claude or Codex executable path is needed: the
            agent invokes the absolute AI Lens command printed by{" "}
            <code>config</code>. Review hook approval and workspace trust in
            your client; generated configuration does not bypass consent.
          </p>
          <details class="command-details" open>
            <summary>Windows · PowerShell</summary>
            <CodeBlock
              code={windowsStart}
              lang="powershell"
              label="PowerShell · start and print configuration"
            />
          </details>
          <details class="command-details">
            <summary>Linux & macOS · POSIX shell</summary>
            <CodeBlock
              code={posixStart}
              label="Shell · start and print configuration"
            />
          </details>
          <div class="callout">
            <strong>
              <code>config</code> prints. You review and merge.
            </strong>
            <p>
              It does not edit settings, approve trust, or start the agent.
              Preserve existing hook entries and follow the agent's own reload
              and consent requirements.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="integration-list">
            <article>
              <span class="integration-label">CLAUDE CODE</span>
              <h3>Merge into the settings hooks.</h3>
              <p>
                Use <code>--agent claude</code>. Review the generated{" "}
                <code>hooks</code> object for <code>.claude/settings.json</code>{" "}
                or your user settings. On Windows, the command above requests
                PowerShell quoting.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span class="integration-label">CODEX</span>
              <h3>Review native hooks JSON.</h3>
              <p>
                Run the same config command with <code>--agent codex</code>.
                Merge its entries into <code>.codex/hooks.json</code> or{" "}
                <code>~/.codex/hooks.json</code>; review the definitions through
                the client's hook interface.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span class="integration-label">HERMES</span>
              <h3>Use native synchronous shell hooks.</h3>
              <p>
                Use <code>--agent hermes</code>. Merge the generated mapping
                into the profile's <code>config.yaml</code>. Hermes uses native
                argv quoting on each OS, independently of <code>--shell</code>;
                do not add PowerShell's <code>&amp;</code> operator. Complete
                Hermes's hook consent normally.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span class="integration-label">GEMINI CLI</span>
              <h3>Merge native settings hooks.</h3>
              <p>
                Use <code>--agent gemini</code>. Merge the
                generated hooks into <code>~/.gemini/settings.json</code> or a
                trusted project's <code>.gemini/settings.json</code>. Follow the
                <a href="#gemini">Gemini registration steps below</a>.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
          <p>
            Claude/Codex prevention covers supported <code>PreToolUse</code> and{" "}
            <code>PermissionRequest</code> events. Hermes uses{" "}
            <code>pre_tool_call</code>; Gemini CLI uses <code>BeforeTool</code>.
            Verify your installed client/version and
            the exact tool surface in the{" "}
            <a href={source("docs/agents.md")}>adapter coverage notes</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Register hooks where the tools execute. A local hook does not cover
            a separate cloud runner. Test an allowed call and a denied, harmless
            side effect through your actual client. <code>status</code> reports
            local state; it does not probe agent registration or event delivery.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="doc-section" id="gemini">
          <p class="eyebrow">FOURTH ADAPTER / GEMINI CLI</p>
          <h2>Register Gemini's native hooks.</h2>
          <p>
            Download and extract a package, then run these commands from its directory.
            Keep the executable beside its engine library. You can also
            <a href="#source">build and package the source</a> yourself.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock
            code={`lens_state="$PWD/lens-state"
./agent-lens --state "$lens_state" start \\
  --output "$PWD/events.jsonl" --rules "$PWD/examples/rules/default.yaml"
./agent-lens --state "$lens_state" config --agent gemini`}
            label="Gemini CLI · Linux & macOS package"
          />
          <CodeBlock
            code={`$lensState = Join-Path $PWD 'lens-state'
$lensOutput = Join-Path $PWD 'events.jsonl'
$lensRules = Join-Path $PWD 'examples\\rules\\default.yaml'
.\\agent-lens.exe --state $lensState start --output $lensOutput --rules $lensRules
.\\agent-lens.exe --state $lensState config --agent gemini`}
            lang="powershell"
            label="Gemini CLI · Windows package"
          />
          <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Review and merge the printed hooks.</strong>
              <p>
                Use <code>~/.gemini/settings.json</code> or the trusted project's
                <code>.gemini/settings.json</code>. Preserve existing settings
                and hook entries. Each generated command has a timeout of
                <code>10000</code> milliseconds, or ten seconds.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Review the registration in Gemini CLI.</strong>
              <p>
                Open the intended workspace and use <code>/hooks panel</code>.
                Complete native project-hook review and any workspace trust
                prompts; changed project hooks may need review again. Agent
                Lens does not install Gemini, configure it automatically, or
                change trust and permissions.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Check an allowed and a denied harmless call.</strong>
              <p>
                Inspect both the actual side effect and the JSONL. A printed
                config or Lens <code>status</code> does not confirm registration.
                Local replay and offline dispatcher checks do not establish an
                authenticated Google live-client result.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The inspected Gemini CLI 0.66.0 runtime shell helper selects
            PowerShell on Windows and <code>bash -c</code> on Unix. This describes
            runtime components, not verified whole-client compatibility. AI Lens selects native quoting
            independently of <code>--shell</code>. Its nine events are
            <code>BeforeTool</code>, <code>AfterTool</code>, <code>BeforeAgent</code>,
            <code>AfterAgent</code>, <code>BeforeToolSelection</code>,
            <code>SessionStart</code>, <code>SessionEnd</code>,
            <code>Notification</code>, and <code>PreCompress</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>SessionEnd</code> delivery is best effort: upstream shutdown
            does not wait for the hook, so its callback may be lost. Configuring
            all nine events does not guarantee that every event will be observed
            in a live session.
          </p>
          <p>
            Only <code>BeforeTool</code> is enforceable: AI Lens replies with
            <code>decision: "deny"</code> and a generic <code>reason</code> for
            a block. Other matches produce <code>would_block</code> and a neutral
            response; no completed action is undone. <code>BeforeModel</code>
            and <code>AfterModel</code> are excluded to avoid model-request and
            response-chunk overhead. Antigravity is not supported.
          </p>
          <div class="callout">
            <strong>Whole-client compatibility remains unverified.</strong>
            <p>
              The pinned 0.66.0 native check exercises the unmodified vendor
              runtime/tool seam, including the full <code>HookSystem</code>, with
              a minimal Config facade and explicitly fired events. It excludes
              actual CLI settings loading, trust/consent, and provider sessions.
              The check is configured in CI for Linux, Windows, and macOS; see
              the <a href={source("docs/verification.md")}>verification record</a>
              and workflow results for observed outcomes. This is not an
              authenticated Google live-client claim.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p>
            Direct path rules cover native <code>file_path</code> arguments in
            <code>read_file</code>, <code>write_file</code>, and <code>replace</code>.
            They do not interpret <code>read_many_files</code> include globs or
            expand them into file paths; that tool is outside this coverage.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read the <a href="https://geminicli.com/docs/hooks/reference/">official hook reference</a>,
            <a href="https://geminicli.com/docs/hooks/">configuration and hook review</a>,
            <a href="https://geminicli.com/docs/cli/trusted-folders/">workspace trust guidance</a>,
            and <a href={source("docs/agents.md")}>adapter coverage notes</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="doc-section" id="cowork">
          <p class="eyebrow">COWORK</p>
          <h2>Upload a plugin. Start a task.</h2>
          <p>Download the <a href={releaseAsset(`ai-lens-cowork-${releaseVersion}.zip`)}>Cowork plugin ZIP</a>,
            then open Customize → Plugins → Add → Upload plugin in Claude Desktop.
            Enable AI Lens and start a new Cowork task. Follow normal folder permissions.</p>
          <p>The plugin includes the Linux executable, engine and default rules for Cowork's cloud runtime.
            Try creating <code>hello.txt</code>, then a harmless <code>.env</code> file.
            The first should work; the second should be blocked.</p>
          <p>State and metadata logs stay inside the task runtime. See <a href={source("examples/cowork-plugin/README.md")}>the plugin guide</a>
            for finding the log, exporting it, and turning Lens off or back on.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="doc-section" id="evidence">
          <p class="eyebrow">04 / JSONL TO YOUR WORKFLOW</p>
          <h2>Take the context with you.</h2>
          <p>
            Normal events contain the agent, native hook/tool identifiers when
            present, phase, decision, matched rule IDs, and enforcement support.
            Configure your existing collector to tail <code>events.jsonl</code>{" "}
            and parse one JSON object per line.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock
            code={`index=agent_lens sourcetype=agent_lens:jsonl decision=block
| stats count by agent, tool_name`}
            lang="text"
            label="Splunk · after collector field mapping"
          />
          <p>
            <code>block</code> records the denial AI Lens requested. Confirm
            actual prevention independently. <code>would_block</code> is
            observational and never means an action was undone. Exclude{" "}
            <code>synthetic: true</code> demo events from live activity counts.
          </p>
          <div class="callout">
            <strong>Keep collection metadata first.</strong>
            <p>
              <code>start --capture-content</code> enables content capture after
              best-effort redaction. Redaction can miss secrets. Even metadata
              can be sensitive; you own permissions, retention, rotation, and
              forwarding.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p>
            AI Lens provides no network shipper, delivery acknowledgment, or
            tamper-evident audit trail. Add trusted host identity at the
            collector and consult the{" "}
            <a href={source("docs/siem.md")}>SIEM mapping and query guide</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="doc-section" id="stop">
          <p class="eyebrow">05 / CLOSE THE LOOP</p>
          <h2>Stop collection with the same state.</h2>
          <CodeBlock
            code={`.\\agent-lens.exe --state $lensState stop`}
            lang="powershell"
            label="Windows · PowerShell"
          />
          <CodeBlock
            code={'./agent-lens --state "$PWD/lens-state" stop'}
            label="Linux & macOS · shell"
          />
          <p>
            This disables collection and enforcement for that state directory.
            It leaves prior events on disk and manually installed hook settings
            in place. Remove those entries yourself when uninstalling; stop
            collection before moving its output file.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="doc-section" id="source">
          <p class="eyebrow">ALTERNATIVE / BUILD IT YOURSELF</p>
          <h2>Build from source.</h2>
          <p>
            Use current stable Rust. Python 3.9+ is needed for the repository's
            checks and packaging, not to run the application. Older Rust
            toolchains are not validated.
          </p>
          <CodeBlock
            code={`git clone ${repository}.git
cd AI-Lens
cargo build --locked --workspace --release
python3 scripts/package.py`}
            label="Source build · POSIX shell"
          />
          <p>
            On Windows, use your Python 3 command in place of{" "}
            <code>python3</code>. The ZIP and manifest appear in{" "}
            <code>dist/</code>. Build both workspace crates so the executable
            has its adjacent dynamic engine library.
          </p>
          <a class="button secondary" href={href("rules/")}>
            Understand the policy schema <Icon name="arrow" size={17} />
          </a>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
